    "summary": "Red Rocks ’82 delivers a high‑energy, psychedelic second set with monster jams",
    "review": "The July 27, 1982 Red Rocks show is pure summer fire – the band rides a relentless wave of energy from start to finish. The first set offers solid classics (Jack Straw, Sugaree, China Cat > Rider) but it’s the second set that really shines: a smoking Wheel > The Other One, an extended 9‑minute “The Other One” that sees Phil’s bass explode, and the tight Playing In The Band → Terrapin Station → Playing sandwich that demonstrates how well the five‑piece listens to each other. Highlights like the soaring China Cat > Rider segue, a heartfelt Stella Blue reprise, and an unforgettable drum break before the Rhythm Devils chant keep the night feeling both psychedelic and deeply musical.\n\nWhile soundboard sources suffer from early hiss and mix imbalances (Jerry’s guitar sometimes buried, Brent’s organ up front), the matrix recording captured by Beyer/M160 is crystal‑clear with excellent mids and minimal audience bleed, making it the definitive listening version. Audience tapes also capture the venue’s natural ambience and showcase Garcia’s bright mic placement, Phil’s booming bass, and a rare Bob Weir scat vocal over “Playing In The Band.” Overall this performance stands out as one of Red Rocks’ most thrilling late‑summer shows.",
